00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:00.220
when something does
actually break.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.240
So let's see how we can maybe
reduce some of that noise.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.129
SETH VARGO: So my partner
and I would greatly

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:07.003
appreciate getting to
sleep through the night

00:01:07.003 --> 00:01:09.870
unless things are
actually on fire.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.210
But I'm worried, too,
because I've heard rumors

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.710
that the rest of
my team is actually

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.200
ignoring alerts and putting
their phones in Do Not Disturb

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.372
mode because none of the
alerts are actionable,

00:01:18.372 --> 00:01:19.830
or very few of them
are actionable.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:20.670
And I'm worried
that that's going

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.294
to put our business at risk
in the event of a real outage.

00:01:23.294 --> 00:01:25.710
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yeah, so you
have service level indicators

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.130
and service level objectives.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:28.020
Remember last time?

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:29.020
SETH VARGO: Yeah, we do.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.850
We have an automated system that
verifies that you can log in,

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.574
you can add things to
your shopping cart,

00:01:34.574 --> 00:01:35.490
and you can check out.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.050
And our service
level indicator is

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.780
defined based on whether
that probe, that process,

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.630
succeeds without error and
whether it completes quickly

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:43.680
enough in our time frame.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.230
And then our service
level objective, or SLO,

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.870
is that integrated over,
say, the past 30 days.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:53.220
And the SLI must be satisfied
for 99.9%, or three nines,

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:54.096
of all of the probes.

00:01:54.096 --> 00:01:56.553
LIZ FONG-JONES: Great, so you're
measuring your performance

00:01:56.553 --> 00:01:57.900
and what users are experiencing.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.510
But are you using that to alert?

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:02.520
SETH VARGO: Wait, you want
me to add more alerts?

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.591
I want to sleep more.

00:02:04.591 --> 00:02:06.090
LIZ FONG-JONES:
Well, yes, but we'll

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:08.490
eventually wind up turning
off the redundant alerts that

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.909
are measuring potential
causes of problems

00:02:10.909 --> 00:02:12.450
rather than
corresponding necessarily

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:14.309
to actual unhappy users.

00:02:14.309 --> 00:02:16.850
So remember, what we're trying
to do is make our users happy.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:20.550
So by using the SLO measurements
to decide whether our users are

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.865
experiencing actual
symptoms of pain,

00:02:22.865 --> 00:02:24.240
then we can use
that to guide us,

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:25.930
and everything
else is extraneous.

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:27.305
SETH VARGO: OK,
that makes sense.

00:02:27.305 --> 00:02:28.800
LIZ FONG-JONES: So
let's take an example.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.716
You said you wanted to
have your SLI satisfied

00:02:30.716 --> 00:02:33.241
99.9% over 30 days.

00:02:33.241 --> 00:02:34.740
So that means that
you have an error

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.010
budget of 43 minutes per
30 days of 100% outage,

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.540
or potentially longer if you
had a partial brownout, right?

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.060
SETH VARGO: Right, that
math sounds familiar.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.470
LIZ FONG-JONES:
OK, so you'd want

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.060
to find out and be
able to intervene

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.370
to put the system back
to correctness relatively

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.844
quickly if you are expending
error budget far more

00:02:49.844 --> 00:02:51.510
quickly than the error
budget indicates.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.559
For instance, you'd
clearly have a problem

00:02:53.559 --> 00:02:55.350
if you spent a quarter
of your error budget

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.450
for the whole month
within two hours,

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.880
either by being hard
down for 10 minutes

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.640
or by having a partial
brownout of 10% of your probes

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:03.500
for those two hours.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:04.500
SETH VARGO: Well, I see.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.200
And if that pattern continued
for the next few hours, or even

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.180
minutes, I could
potentially wind up

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.781
spending my entire error budget
for the quarter or the year.

00:03:11.781 --> 00:03:12.780
LIZ FONG-JONES: Exactly.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.550
But we can't put it
on a hair trigger

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.410
or else it will
falsely alert us when

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.470
we have a blip for two
minutes, rather than something

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:22.060
that winds up repeating itself
within the next two hours.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.025
So we can have some
situations where

00:03:24.025 --> 00:03:25.650
a partial brownout
wouldn't necessarily

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.090
endanger our error budget.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.430
SETH VARGO: Yeah, I've
had enough noisy pages

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:31.560
to want to avoid that
happening ever again.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.500
But I think I can spot a
small hole in your logic.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.650
Like, what if I deployed
some code that introduced,

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:38.400
say, a small
performance regression?

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.190
Something maybe half of
a percent of my probes

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:42.600
will constantly fail.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.500
I'd never catch it by
looking for too many failures

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.530
in a window of hours.

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:49.156
That would be indistinguishable
from other noise in the system.

00:03:49.156 --> 00:03:50.988
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yes,
you're exactly correct.

00:03:50.988 --> 00:03:52.560
So we also need
a slow burn alert

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.620
to track down over the past week
if we're exceeding our error

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.840
budget at an acceptable rate.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.630
For instance, we could check
whether more than 0.15%

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:03.441
of queries were failing when
we're only allowed 0.1%.

00:04:03.441 --> 00:04:05.190
And we could raise a
lower priority ticket

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.190
that someone could look
at during business hours

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:08.664
once we are
sufficiently confident.

00:04:08.664 --> 00:04:10.080
SETH VARGO: So as
I understand it,

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.570
we need both fast burn
and slow burn alerts

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:14.769
to provide a feedback
loop on our SLO

00:04:14.769 --> 00:04:16.769
and make sure that we're
fixing real issues that

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:17.839
are affecting real users.

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:18.839
LIZ FONG-JONES: Exactly.

00:04:18.839 --> 00:04:20.910
And Stackdriver SLO
from Google Cloud

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:24.570
can help you by automatically
determining and showing to you

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.820
what your error budget is,
how much of it you spend,

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:29.014
and alerting you
when it's in danger.

00:04:29.014 --> 00:04:30.930
And once you've set up
these SLO-based alerts,

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:32.520
you can turn off all
of the noisy alerts

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:33.840
because you're
only getting alerts

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.090
about the high importance
problems that are actually

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:37.131
causing user impact.

00:04:37.131 --> 00:04:38.880
SETH VARGO: OK, but
how do I actually find

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.620
the problems in my
system so that I

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:44.630
feel confident turning off
the no longer needed alerts?

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.600
I'm worried that I'm
going to lose data or be

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:47.721
unable to fix issues now.

00:04:47.721 --> 00:04:50.220
LIZ FONG-JONES: That sounds
like an excellent subject for us

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.950
to discuss on the next
episode about observability

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:54.640
